-- title:
--   Node integers have q-expansions over a number field
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a nonzero level $N$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data $data$ for $q$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair $(j, j_q)$) satisfying the Kronecker congruence $hKr$, and hypotheses $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ asserting that the two Hecke maps $\mathrm{heckeAlphaBar}$, $\mathrm{heckeBetaBar}$ at level $N$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are integral ring homomorphisms. Let $P$ be a place specialization for these data and let $R$ be a prolongation tuple over $P$. Let $w$ be a place of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ N$ over $k$, and let $g$ be an element of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(Nq)$ lying in $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegers}\ w$, that is: $g$ lies in the valuation subring $R.R_1.\mathrm{integers}$, in $R.R_2.\mathrm{integers}$, and in the valuation subring of every place $V$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(Nq)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\ V = w$. Finally let $K_0$ be a finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Then there is a finite extension $K$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $K_0 \le K$ and $g \in R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$, i.e. $g$ lies in $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegers}\ w$ and its Laurent expansion lies in the subfield $\mathrm{NodeLocalized.fieldOver}\ (Nq)\ K$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$, generated by the scalars from $K$ together with the two level-$Nq$ modular generators.
--
--   This is the descent step saying that membership in the node ring at $(w, \varphi w)$ can always be refined to membership in its subring of functions defined over a number field, enlarging any prescribed number field $K_0$. It is used in the analysis of residues at the nodes, namely by the normalisation statement producing a unit multiple with prescribed order and by the compatibility of the node residue with $red$.
-- source:
